import{_ as t,o as n,c as o,a2 as a}from"./chunks/framework.G0y0PQA7.js";const s="/relio/shots/en/dialogs-table.png",r="/relio/shots/en/dialogs-column.png",i="/relio/shots/en/dialogs-relation.png",y=JSON.parse('{"title":"Properties dialogs","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/features/dialogs.md","filePath":"en/features/dialogs.md"}'),l={name:"en/features/dialogs.md"};function d(h,e,c,g,p,u){return n(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="properties-dialogs" tabindex="-1">Properties dialogs <a class="header-anchor" href="#properties-dialogs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Properties dialogs&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The fields of the dialogs that edit objects on the canvas — table, column, Quick entry, index, view, relationship, note connection and image properties. Dialogs opened from the Model menu, such as the word dictionary, groups, sequences and options, are covered in <a href="/relio/en/features/model">Model menu</a>.</p><p>A properties dialog opens when you double-click a node on the canvas, or select it and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>. Double-clicking a table or view in the outline also opens it, and double-clicking a column line under a table opens the table properties dialog with that column selected. For the steps to add columns, see <a href="/relio/en/quick/draw">Draw tables, columns and relationships</a> in Quick start.</p><h2 id="table-properties" tabindex="-1">Table properties <a class="header-anchor" href="#table-properties" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Table properties&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Logical name</strong> and <strong>Physical name</strong> are at the top, with six tabs below.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Tab</th><th>Contents</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Attributes</td><td>The column table (primary key (PK) · foreign key (FK) · physical name · logical name · type · NOT NULL · UNIQUE) with <strong>Add</strong> · <strong>Edit</strong> · <strong>Delete</strong> · up and down · <strong>Quick entry</strong>. Below it: a field to choose a group, <strong>Add group items to the table</strong>, the group column table and <strong>Manage groups…</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Description</td><td>A multi-line description used in documents and comments</td></tr><tr><td>Composite unique keys</td><td>The key list, <strong>UNIQUE KEY constraint name</strong>, a column checklist, and <strong>Add</strong> · <strong>Update</strong> · <strong>Delete</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Constraints/options</td><td><strong>Table constraint</strong>, <strong>Primary key constraint name</strong>, <strong>Options</strong> (appended at the end of the table in the DDL)</td></tr><tr><td>Index</td><td>The index list with <strong>Add</strong> · <strong>Edit</strong> · <strong>Delete</strong> — see &quot;Index properties&quot; below</td></tr><tr><td>Advanced settings</td><td>Per-database table options (schema, tablespace; for MySQL also storage engine, character set, collation, length when a TEXT type is a primary key and so on)</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>The fields in the column table (physical name, logical name, type, NOT NULL, UNIQUE) can be edited right in the table. Double-click a row, or select it and press <kbd>Space</kbd>, to open the column properties dialog.</li><li>If you open the dialog with a column selected on the canvas, that row is selected.</li><li>While you type, a line under the field points out problems (nothing is blocked). <a href="/relio/en/features/check#notes-where-you-type">Design check and suggestions › Notes where you type</a></li><li><strong>Columns that cannot be deleted</strong> — FK columns (delete the relationship instead) and columns referenced by other tables cannot be deleted. In that case, <strong>Delete with relationships</strong> deletes them together with the relationships; pressing OK applies it in one step, and it can be undone.</li><li>A composite unique key needs at least one column, and you cannot create two keys with the same column combination. Constraint names may use only letters, digits and <code>_</code>; leave it empty to create the key without a name (the database assigns one).</li></ul><figure class="shot" data-scene="dialogs-table"><img src="'+s+'" alt="Table properties — the column table on the Attributes tab" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Table properties — the column table on the Attributes tab</figcaption></figure><h2 id="column-properties" tabindex="-1">Column properties <a class="header-anchor" href="#column-properties" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Column properties&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="general-tab" tabindex="-1">General tab <a class="header-anchor" href="#general-tab" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;General tab&quot;">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Primary key · NOT NULL · UNIQUE · AUTO_INCREMENT</td><td>Turning on Primary key also turns on NOT NULL and locks it. AUTO_INCREMENT can be turned on only for a primary key with an integer type, and only if the table has no other auto increment column.</td></tr><tr><td>Word</td><td>Choosing a word from the word dictionary fills the names, type, length and description with that word&#39;s values. The <strong>Filter</strong> field filters the list. Leave it empty to create a new word from the values you enter.</td></tr><tr><td>Logical name · Physical name</td><td>When you type only the physical name, the logical name follows it. Once you edit the logical name yourself, the two go separate ways. <strong>Suggested physical name</strong> uses the translation dictionary to suggest a physical name from the logical name; <strong>Apply</strong> puts it in.</td></tr><tr><td>Type · Length · Decimal</td><td>The type list uses the same common type names as ERMaster. If the type is generated under a different name for the current database, a note under the field says which type it becomes in that database. Fields that a type does not take (length or scale) are dimmed. FK columns follow the parent column&#39;s type.</td></tr><tr><td>UNSIGNED · ZEROFILL · BINARY</td><td>Shown only for MySQL and MariaDB.</td></tr><tr><td>enum/set arguments</td><td>The list of values when the type is enum or set.</td></tr><tr><td>Default value</td><td>Type a value, or choose <strong>Current date/time</strong> or <strong>Empty string</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>Description</td><td>The description used in documents and comments.</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Columns that use a word</strong> — When a column uses a word, editing its names or type changes the word itself, so every column that uses the same word changes too. &quot;Apply to n columns using this word&quot; appears next to the field; to change only this column, click <strong>Split this column into a new word</strong>.</p><p><strong>Suggestions while typing</strong> — Type a physical or logical name and candidates appear above the field. Pick one with <kbd>↓</kbd> and accept it with <kbd>Enter</kbd>. If you accepted the wrong one, click <strong>Undo fill</strong>. See <a href="/relio/en/features/check#suggestions-while-typing">Design check and suggestions</a> for details.</p><h3 id="properties-tab" tabindex="-1">Properties tab <a class="header-anchor" href="#properties-tab" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Properties tab&quot;">​</a></h3><p>This tab has <strong>UNIQUE KEY constraint name</strong> · <strong>Column constraint</strong> · <strong>Character set</strong> · <strong>Collation</strong>, and the <strong>AUTO_INCREMENT settings…</strong> button used when AUTO_INCREMENT is on. The AUTO_INCREMENT settings dialog shows only the values that database uses (start and increment for MySQL, MariaDB, SQL Server and MS Access; start for Informix; start, increment, min value, max value, cache and cycle (CYCLE) for the others). SQLite does not have this button.</p><figure class="shot" data-scene="dialogs-column"><img src="'+r+'" alt="Column properties — the word, name and type fields on the General tab" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Column properties — the word, name and type fields on the General tab</figcaption></figure><h2 id="quick-entry" tabindex="-1">Quick entry <a class="header-anchor" href="#quick-entry" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Quick entry&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Add many columns at once in a single grid. The columns are logical name · physical name · type · length · decimal, and you can paste a table copied from Excel into the first cell. <strong>Add row</strong> adds more lines. Quick entry is available in the table properties, view properties and Manage groups dialogs.</p><h2 id="index-properties" tabindex="-1">Index properties <a class="header-anchor" href="#index-properties" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Index properties&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>UNIQUE</strong> and <strong>FULL TEXT</strong> (MySQL and MariaDB only) checkboxes, <strong>Table name</strong> (read-only), <strong>Index name</strong>, <strong>Index type</strong> (only the methods that database supports) and <strong>Description</strong>.</li><li>Pick columns from <strong>All columns</strong> on the left and use <strong>Add ▶</strong> / <strong>◀ Remove</strong> to put them in <strong>Index columns (column name · descending)</strong> on the right; set the order with <strong>Up</strong> · <strong>Down</strong>. Descending order can be used on every supported database.</li><li>If you leave the index name empty and choose columns, a name is made for you.</li></ul><h2 id="view-properties" tabindex="-1">View properties <a class="header-anchor" href="#view-properties" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;View properties&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The tabs are <strong>Attributes</strong> (the same column table as table properties) · <strong>SQL</strong> · <strong>Description</strong> · <strong>Advanced settings</strong>. The SQL field has line numbers and syntax coloring, and you cannot press OK while the SQL is empty. <strong>Get columns from SQL</strong> reads the SELECT list and fills the column table.</p><h2 id="relationship-properties" tabindex="-1">Relationship properties <a class="header-anchor" href="#relationship-properties" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Relationship properties&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Opens when you double-click a relationship line.</p><ul><li><strong>Constraint name</strong> — Leave it empty to create the constraint without a name (the database assigns one).</li><li><strong>ON UPDATE</strong> · <strong>ON DELETE</strong> — RESTRICT · CASCADE · NO ACTION · SET NULL · SET DEFAULT. If you choose an action the current database does not support, a note next to the field says &quot;Output as NO ACTION in this DB&quot; or &quot;Omitted in this DB (not output)&quot;.</li><li><strong>Parent</strong> — The referenced table (read-only), the <strong>Referenced target</strong> (the primary key or a composite unique key) and <strong>Cardinality</strong> (1 · 0..1).</li><li><strong>Child</strong> — <strong>Cardinality</strong> (1..n · 0..n · 1 · 0..1) and the list of <strong>FK columns</strong>.</li></ul><p>Changing the referenced target rebuilds the child&#39;s FK columns to match the new target (one undo step). When you delete a relationship, Relio asks whether to delete its FK columns too.</p><figure class="shot" data-scene="dialogs-relation"><img src="'+i+'" alt="Relationship properties — ON UPDATE, ON DELETE and cardinality" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Relationship properties — ON UPDATE, ON DELETE and cardinality</figcaption></figure><h3 id="relationship-using-existing-columns" tabindex="-1">Relationship using existing columns <a class="header-anchor" href="#relationship-using-existing-columns" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Relationship using existing columns&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Opens when you click parent → child with <strong>1:N relationship using existing columns</strong> in the palette. Choose the <strong>Referenced target (parent primary key)</strong>, then pick the <strong>Child FK columns</strong> for each target column in the table. Columns with matching names are picked in advance and marked &quot;Picked by name&quot;. Choosing a column with a different type shows a warning.</p><h2 id="note-connections-and-images" tabindex="-1">Note connections and images <a class="header-anchor" href="#note-connections-and-images" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Note connections and images&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Connection properties</strong> — Opens when you double-click a note connection line. Choose a <strong>Line color</strong> or go back to the <strong>Default color</strong>.</li><li><strong>Image properties</strong> — Opens when you double-click an image. <strong>Hue (°)</strong> · <strong>Saturation (%)</strong> · <strong>Brightness (%)</strong> · <strong>Opacity (0–255)</strong> and <strong>Keep aspect ratio</strong>. Changes show in the preview right away.</li></ul>',31)])])}const b=t(l,[["render",d]]);export{y as __pageData,b as default};
